    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find \\(\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4)\\), we can use a property of determinants that states the determinant of a matrix raised to a power is the determinant of the matrix raised to that power. Mathematically, this is expressed as:\n\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^n) = (\\det \\mathbf{M})^n\n\\]\n\nGiven that \\(\\det \\mathbf{M} = -2\\), we need to find \\(\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4)\\). Using the property mentioned above, we substitute \\(n = 4\\):\n\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4) = (\\det \\mathbf{M})^4\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute the given value of \\(\\det \\mathbf{M}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4) = (-2)^4\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\((-2)^4\\):\n\n\\[\n(-2)^4 = (-2) \\times (-2) \\times (-2) \\times (-2)\n\\]\n\nFirst, we multiply the first two:\n\n\\[\n(-2) \\times (-2) = 4\n\\]\n\nThen we multiply the result by the next two:\n\n\\[\n4 \\times (-2) = -8\n\\]\n\n\\[\n-8 \\times (-2) = 16\n\\]\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4) = 16\n\\]\n\nPutting the final answer in the required format, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{16}\n\\]",
